2. Angular 21 Modern Features Deep Dive
Instructor Cue: "Before we build, let's understand the three game-changing features that make Angular 21 so powerful."
A. Standalone Components (No More NgModules)


Old Way (Angular 16 and earlier):
// user.module.ts
@NgModule({
   declarations: [UserComponent],
   imports: [CommonModule, FormsModule],
   exports: [UserComponent]
})
export class UserModule {}


// user.component.ts
@Component({ ... })
export class UserComponent {}


New Way (Angular 21):
// user.component.ts
@Component({
   selector: 'app-user',
   standalone: true,  // ← That's it!
   imports: [CommonModule, FormsModule],  // Import directly
   templateUrl: './user.component.html'
})
export class UserComponent {}


Why this matters:
* No more module files cluttering your project
* Easier to understand (imports are right where you use them)
* Better tree-shaking (only import what you need)
* Faster learning curve for beginners
B. Signals: The New Reactive Primitive
Signals are reactive values that automatically update the UI when they change.
import { signal, computed, effect } from '@angular/core';


// Create a signal
const count = signal(0);


// Read the value
console.log(count());  // 0


// Update the value
count.set(5);
count.update(value => value + 1);  // 6


// Computed signal (derived value)
const doubleCount = computed(() => count() * 2);
console.log(doubleCount());  // 12


// Effect (side effect when signal changes)
effect(() => {
   console.log(`Count changed to: ${count()}`);
});


Why Signals over RxJS for auth state?
RxJS BehaviorSubject
	Signal
	currentUser$ (Observable)
	currentUser (Signal)
	currentUser$.subscribe(...)
	currentUser()
	currentUser$.next(user)
	currentUser.set(user)
	Complex operators
	Simple, intuitive API
	Need async pipe in templates
	Auto-tracks in templates
	

For authentication state, Signals are perfect because:
* Auth state is simple (logged in or not, current user)
* No complex stream operations needed
* Templates auto-update when signal changes
* Easier to test and reason about
C. Built-in Control Flow
Old Way (Directives):
<div *ngIf="isLoggedIn">
   <p>Welcome, {{ user.name }}</p>
</div>


<ul>
   <li *ngFor="let task of tasks">
       {{ task.title }}
   </li>
</ul>


New Way (Built-in Control Flow):
if (isLoggedIn) {
   <p>Welcome, {{ user().name }}</p>
}


<ul>
   @for (task of tasks; track task.id) {
       <li>{{ task.title }}</li>
   } @empty {
       <li>No tasks yet</li>
   }
</ul>


Why this is better:
* No more *ngIf, *ngFor, *ngSwitch directives
* Cleaner, more readable templates
* Better performance (no directive overhead)
* Feels like modern JavaScript (similar to JSX/Svelte)
D. Deferrable Views (@defer)
Lazy-load parts of your template for better performance:
<!-- Heavy component loads only when visible -->
@defer (on viewport) {
   <app-expensive-chart />
} @placeholder {
   <p>Loading chart...</p>
} @loading {
   <app-spinner />
}


Key Takeaway: Angular 21 is built around three pillars: Standalone Components, Signals, and Built-in Control Flow. Master these, and you master modern Angular.
